---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ProjectCard from '../../components/ProjectCard.astro';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Work"
  description="Case studies and selected projects by Eric Nolan across AI engineering, developer tooling, practical software, and product design."
>
  <header class="page-header">
    <div class="shell">
      <p class="eyebrow">Work</p>
      <h1>Products, systems, and the decisions behind them.</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        Public repositories where openness makes sense. Public-safe case studies where the product or data should remain private. The evidence matters more than repository visibility.
      </p>
    </div>
  </header>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="shell">
      <div class="section-heading">
        <div>
          <p class="eyebrow">Featured case studies</p>
          <h2>Different problems. Consistent judgment.</h2>
        </div>
        <p class="section-intro">
          The set intentionally mixes AI infrastructure with end-user products. I do not want the portfolio to imply that every useful problem needs an agent.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="project-grid">
        <ProjectCard
          title="Personal Memory Bank"
          href="/work/personal-memory-bank/"
          eyebrow="AI engineering"
          description="Persistent project memory governed by authority, freshness, provenance, hooks, diagnostics, review workflows, and CI."
          status="Open source"
          tags={['Context', 'Governance', 'CLI', 'Developer productivity']}
        />
        <ProjectCard
          title="AI Code Review Agent"
          href="/work/ai-code-review-agent/"
          eyebrow="Developer tooling"
          description="Local multi-agent review with specialist isolation, confidence-aware findings, privacy-conscious inference, and multiple developer interfaces."
          status="Open source"
          tags={['Ollama', 'Review', 'Security', 'Verification']}
          tone="slate"
        />
        <ProjectCard
          title="Side Quest Atlas"
          href="/work/side-quest-atlas/"
          eyebrow="Travel memory product"
          description="Private small-group travel memory capture designed around 'capture now, enrich later,' group ownership, and clear access boundaries."
          status="Private case study"
          tags={['Flutter', 'Supabase', 'RLS', 'Product modeling']}
          tone="gold"
        />
        <ProjectCard
          title="PitLogic"
          href="/work/pitlogic/"
          eyebrow="Telemetry product"
          description="Client-side BBQ telemetry and analytics with live device integration, alerts, stall detection, forecasting, history, and data portability."
          status="Open source"
          tags={['React', 'PWA', 'Telemetry', 'Analytics']}
          tone="copper"
        />
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section--warm">
    <div class="shell">
      <div class="section-heading">
        <div>
          <p class="eyebrow">Private, not hidden</p>
          <h2>Some products are better documented than published.</h2>
        </div>
        <p class="section-intro">
          Privacy, family data, club history, and user-specific records are legitimate reasons to keep a repository closed. The case studies expose the product decisions without exposing the people or data.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="project-grid">
        <ProjectCard
          title="Tipsy Bunghole"
          href="/work/tipsy-bunghole/"
          eyebrow="Live event workflow"
          description="A private whiskey-club product that coordinates blind tasting events across participant, steward, and host roles while preserving institutional memory."
          status="Private case study"
          tags={['React', 'Supabase', 'Roles', 'Event state']}
          tone="copper"
        />
        <ProjectCard
          title="Bowling Tracker"
          href="/work/bowling-tracker/"
          eyebrow="Sports tracking"
          description="A focused bowling product for sessions, scoring, equipment, patterns, leagues, drills, and analytics—designed around the bowler's actual workflow."
          status="Private case study"
          tags={['Flutter', 'Offline sync', 'Analytics', 'Domain rules']}
          tone="slate"
        />
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section--dark">
    <div class="shell split-feature">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">Research</p>
        <h2>Harness Engineering</h2>
        <p class="section-intro">
          The projects above repeatedly exposed the same deeper problem: AI quality depends on much more than the model. Harness Engineering is where I work through those surrounding systems explicitly.
        </p>
        <p><a class="text-link" style="color:#fff;" href="/harness-engineering/">Read the research overview →</a></p>
      </div>
      <aside class="feature-note" style="background:rgba(255,255,255,.06); border-color:rgba(255,255,255,.14);">
        <strong style="color:#fff;">A framework, not a claim of doctrine.</strong>
        <p>It is evolving public research grounded in observed engineering problems, reference implementations, and explicit tradeoffs.</p>
      </aside>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
